To determine the deBroglie wavelength (\(\lambda_{p}\)) of the electron, we use the formula:
\[
\lambda_{p} = \frac{h}{p}
\]
where \(h\) is Planck's constant (\(6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{s}\)) and \(p\) is the momentum of the electron. The momentum \(p\) can be related to the kinetic energy \(E\) and the mass \(m\) of the electron by the equation:
\[
p = \sqrt{2 E / m}
\]
Given the kinetic energy \(E = 10 \, \text{eV} = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J}\) and the mass \(m = 9.11 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\), we substitute these values into the momentum equation:
\[
p = \sqrt{2 \times 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J} / 9.11 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}} = \sqrt{2 \times 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \times 10^{31}}{9.11 \times 10^{-31}}
\]
\[
p = \sqrt{3.2 \times 10^{12}}{9.11 \times 10^{-31}} = \sqrt{3.52 \times 10^{43}}{9.11 \times 10^{-31}}
\]
\[
p \approx 3.91 \times 10^{22} \, \text{kg} \cdot \text{m/s}
\]
Now, substituting \(p\) into the deBroglie wavelength formula:
\[
\lambda_{p} = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{s}}{3.91 \times 10^{22} \, \text{kg} \cdot \text{m/s}}
\]
\[
\lambda_{p} \approx 1.68 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m}
\]

